WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.157
What you see rotating in the center of your screen is the International Space Station — to scale, every truss, every solar array, every pressurized module.

00:00:11.157 --> 00:00:15.619
At its full length, ISS is one hundred nine meters end to end.

00:00:15.619 --> 00:00:16.843
A football pitch.

00:00:16.843 --> 00:00:17.922
Drag to rotate.

00:00:17.922 --> 00:00:19.722
Each module is clickable.

00:00:19.722 --> 00:00:27.423
Click any one, and the panel opens with its builder, its launch vehicle, its launch date, and its function.

00:00:27.423 --> 00:00:29.223
Start at the Russian end.

00:00:29.223 --> 00:00:34.477
Zarya — the Functional Cargo Block — was the first module of the station.

00:00:34.477 --> 00:00:41.891
Built by Khrunichev for Russia, paid for by NASA, launched on a Proton-K rocket on November 20th, 1998.

00:00:41.891 --> 00:00:51.968
Zarya provided power, propulsion, and attitude control for the first two years, while the rest of the station was being assembled around it.

00:00:51.968 --> 00:00:53.479
Move forward in time.

00:00:53.479 --> 00:00:57.150
Unity — also called Node 1 — was the second module.

00:00:57.150 --> 00:01:00.317
Launched on Endeavour on December 4th, 1998.

00:01:00.317 --> 00:01:07.875
Two weeks later, Endeavour's crew connected Zarya to Unity in the first on-orbit assembly of the station.

00:01:07.875 --> 00:01:08.883
Click Destiny.

00:01:08.883 --> 00:01:12.266
Destiny is the United States laboratory module.

00:01:12.266 --> 00:01:19.535
Launched on STS-98 in 2001, it has been the primary American science workspace for over twenty years.

00:01:19.535 --> 00:01:20.615
Click Columbus.

00:01:20.615 --> 00:01:25.581
That is the European laboratory module, launched by Atlantis in 2008.

00:01:25.581 --> 00:01:26.373
Click Kibo.

00:01:26.373 --> 00:01:35.010
That is the Japanese experiment module — the largest single ISS module, launched in three flights between 2008 and 2009.

00:01:35.010 --> 00:01:36.810
Look at the solar arrays.

00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:44.727
Eight of them — the original four installed between 2000 and 2009, four more rolled out between 2021 and 2023.

00:01:44.727 --> 00:01:54.300
Together they produce about two hundred forty kilowatts of electrical power — enough for a hundred and twenty average American homes.

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:57.036
Look at the trusses connecting it all.

00:01:57.036 --> 00:02:01.138
The integrated truss structure is the station's skeleton.

00:02:01.138 --> 00:02:06.537
It was assembled segment by segment by Shuttle crews between 2000 and 2011.

00:02:06.537 --> 00:02:12.223
No spacecraft has carried any of those segments since the Shuttle's retirement.

00:02:12.223 --> 00:02:18.197
If a new truss segment were needed today, no current launch vehicle could carry it.

00:02:18.197 --> 00:02:20.284
Eighteen pressurized modules.

00:02:20.284 --> 00:02:21.580
Eighteen builders.

00:02:21.580 --> 00:02:24.747
Eighteen launch dates between 1998 and 2021.

00:02:24.747 --> 00:02:30.145
Two hundred and seventy people from twenty-six countries have lived inside.

00:02:30.145 --> 00:02:31.369
Click any module.

00:02:31.369 --> 00:02:34.320
Read who made it, when, and what it does.

